Your .env already points DATABASE_URL at the Docker MySQL service (mysql:3306 / azura_db). Start the whole stack with Compose so the app container can reach that hostname.

From the project root:


docker compose up --build
That starts two containers:

App (azura_app) on http://localhost:3000. On startup it installs dependencies if the named node_modules volume is empty, runs npm run db:migrate:deploy, then npm run dev.
MySQL 8.4 (azura_mysql) on the host at 127.0.0.1:3307. Inside the Docker network the app uses hostname mysql and port 3306.
Compose waits until MySQL is healthy before starting the app. The first run is slow because of the image build and npm ci.

Leave this terminal running. Detached mode:


docker compose up -d --build
docker compose logs -f app
Stop everything with docker compose down. That keeps the database volume. docker compose down -v deletes it.

Database login (from docker-compose.yml): user azura, database azura_db, host 127.0.0.1, port 3307. The password is MYSQL_PASSWORD in that file. Prisma Studio from the host:


npm run db:studio
If the app cannot connect, DATABASE_URL in .env must use the Compose hostname, not localhost:


mysql://azura:PASSWORD@mysql:3306/azura_db
localhost:3307 only works when the Next.js process runs on your Mac, not inside the app container.

After a package.json change, the azura_node_modules volume can stay stale. Recreate it:


docker compose down
docker volume rm azura_azura_node_modules
docker compose up --build
A blank database sends the first visit to /setup to create the admin user.